WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.430
In this video, Martin and I are going to talk about AI and answer the question "Are we there yet?"

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:13.250
And stick around to the end where Jeff and I will be explaining how we use AI in our everyday lives.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.699
Hopefully you've seen some of my security videos on this channel.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:18.790
Well, I sure have.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.590
Well, great.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.140
And I've seen a lot of your AI videos as well.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.140
So we're going to have a conversation about AI.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.369
Are we there yet?

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:34.439
Well, so I think we're kind of there because one definition of AI is basically the simulation of intelligent behavior in computers.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.819
And we've kind of done that.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.030
What do you think, Martin?

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.359
Yes, I would agree with that definition.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.360
But are we there yet?

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.580
Absolutely not.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.460
I don't think so.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:51.059
And the reason I think that is because of something called a AGI, which is an acronym for artificial general intelligence.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:59.107
And this really describes an AI system that is equivalent to a human and is as diverse as a human

00:00:59.107 --> 00:01:02.670
and can do anything that a human can do, at least as well or better.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.529
And I don't think we are there that yet.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.720
Okay, maybe not.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.450
So let's take a look at some examples of where we've come from and where we're going.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.159
So maybe a hypothetical.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.220
Yeah, I've got to good one.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.379
So let's just consider I also write some numbers down here.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.440
Let's see this.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.264
If I put this up and then that's 3..1..2..5.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.413
If I could tell you in my head, just like that, the answer to that math equation, is that intelligence?

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:28.750
Is really impressive.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.230
It's a nice parlor trick.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.860
But, you know, I've got a calculator.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.909
It can do the same thing.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.540
And I'm really not ready to declare that my calculator is sentient.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.299
It's not artificially intelligent, so maybe not there yet, but it's a great example.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:49.660
Well, how about-- I'll tell you a story from my middle school days, my science teacher considered intelligence

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:55.118
would be if you knew all the elements in the periodic table, where they went, what their atomic numbers were,

00:01:55.118 --> 00:01:59.549
how to spell them and their abbreviations, and you could place all of them where it belonged.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.980
And that would have been considered intelligent.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.250
Was she right?

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.190
With the greatest respect to your science teacher, I don't think that's intelligence either.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.590
I think what you've just described there is a database lookup.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.610
Yeah.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.289
How about then, something a bit more advanced than that.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.280
Something that takes years and years to get good at.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.509
Like a chess grand master.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:26.070
Now, think of all of the time that you have to invest to learn all of the patterns, all of the moves, all of a strategy.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:30.879
Is a chess grandmaster a real example of intelligence?

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.999
Yeah, I think most people would say somebody who is the best in the world at chess is a genius.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.520
So you would think, if you're a genius, you're intelligent.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.289
But guess what?

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:46.979
IBM did this trick in 1997 where we created a computer we call Deep Blue.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.839
And Deep Blue was able to beat the best chess player in the world handily.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.449
So, that, already, if that's your bar, we already passed it. Yet again,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.099
we kind of feel like we're left empty.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.530
We're still not feeling there yet.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.240
So what is the classic question, the classic problem to solve?

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.790
You're going to tell me the Turing Test, right?

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.249
I am going to tell you the Turing Test.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.470
That's exactly right.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.539
And what is the Turing Test, for those who don't know?

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:19.590
With the Turing Test, you basically have a user here and they cannot see who it is that they are typing to.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:27.180
So they're typing messages back and forth, and they may be talking to another person, or they may be talking to a computer.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.998
And if it's indistinguishable when they have their communications and they can't tell if

00:03:32.998 --> 00:03:38.310
they're talking to another person or talking to a computer, then we would declare "game over".

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.179
The computer has achieved artificial intelligence.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.120
So if we could ever do that, then we'd be there, right?

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.050
Well, this one is a little bit disputed as to have we passed the Turing Test or not.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:58.034
But in 2014, technically, some people say that we did with a chatbot that simulated a 13 year old boy.

00:03:58.918 --> 00:04:01.079
Well, so that's setting the bar kind of low, 13 year old boy?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.520
You and I used to be one of those.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.739
So I think maybe we want to set the bar higher.

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:13.259
But would we consider other technologies maybe that have raised the bar further towards the Turing Test?

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:19.920
I mean, I talk with chatGPT daily, and sometimes I could be convinced that is a real person.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.680
So I think there have been a lot of advances in that kind of area.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.310
I think so.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.440
I talk to chatGPT regularly, too.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.820
Nobody else will talk to me, so at least, at least that one that hasn't failed me yet.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.009
So that's a good example.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.110
Clearly the bar has been raised.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:42.779
If we ask the question, "Are we there yet or not?" I feel like it's a little bit like we move the finish line every time we get close to it.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.654
As soon as we cross it, then we say, "Yeah, but that's not quite there yet."

00:04:46.654 --> 00:04:52.500
So we kind of had this this sense of, if you look at all of these things together, we have these sort of siloed savants.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.230
We have something that's really great at arithmetic, we have something that's really great at memorization and recall,

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:02.761
something that's great at a narrowly bounded game, that is quite complex,

00:05:02.761 --> 00:05:07.559
but still has very specific rules that can be mathematically described.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:12.154
Or we're talking about a kind of general intelligence, emotional intelligence,

00:05:12.154 --> 00:05:15.149
that would be required to pass the Turing Test and things like that.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:23.579
So we continue to have these developments in individual areas, but putting it all together, that's your AGI.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.606
But I think it's also worth pointing out that the development curve for AI,

00:05:27.606 --> 00:05:34.709
it just seems very recently, even this year, it's just going faster and faster than ever before.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:40.690
And we are getting closer and closer to this idea of general intelligence-- moving away from where we have everything

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:48.494
siloed like Deep Blue, which is deeply siloed to just chess, to now these AI systems that are really getting closer and closer

00:05:48.494 --> 00:05:52.139
to be able to accomplish much more of what we do as humans.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.320
I think you're right.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.449
When I was in school, we talked about artificial intelligence,

00:05:56.449 --> 00:05:59.939
and it was always about something that was about five or ten years away.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.630
And then ten years later, it was another five or ten years away.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.945
But I feel like we're actually narrowing the gap.

00:06:05.945 --> 00:06:12.749
In the last few years, it's been this steep curve toward what we would consider general artificial intelligence.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.670
So, are we there yet?

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.880
No, but we've never been closer.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.330
Awesome.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:20.830
Hey, hey, hey, Jeff!

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:24.800
We didn't we didn't get to the bit where you tell me how you're using AI in everyday life.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:25.800
I did forget that.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.279
So I actually have a self-driving car that brings me to work.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.260
It's basically a computer that takes me places.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.080
How about you?

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:33.080
How do you use AI?

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:37.496
I realize that I'm not a very good photographer, but I am much better at AI prompting,

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:42.600
so I just prompt what I want in a picture and it generates a beautiful professional photograph. It's fantastic.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.853
Great! And I bet those watching this video don't realize that in fact, you and I aren't here,

00:06:46.853 --> 00:06:50.999
all of this has been scripted by a chatbot and we've been replaced.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:56.410
How about you?

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.899
How are you using AI in your everyday life?

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:00.339
Let us know in the comments.

